Test method: testBugReproduction

Bug summary: Embedded Runge-Kutta adaptive integrators (e.g. Dormand-Prince 8(5,3)) compute the initial step size automatically but don't truncate it to the integration range. With a very short range, the function is evaluated outside the range and the integrator then fails to stop properly. Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer integrators already truncate the first step.

Concrete examples:
[{'input': 'Use an embedded RK integrator such as DormandPrince853Integrator with no user-supplied initial step. Integrate an ODE over an extremely short interval, e.g. from t0=0 to t=1e-10 (much shorter than the automatically computed first step).', 'expected': 'The automatically computed first step is truncated to the integration range, so the ODE is only evaluated within [t0, t] and integration ends exactly at t.', 'actual': 'The first step is not checked against the range, so the ODE function is evaluated at times beyond the end of the range. The integrator then tries to step back and fails to stop correctly.'}, {'input': 'Run the same short-range integration with a Gragg-Bulirsch-Stoer integrator.', 'expected': 'The first step is truncated to the range, which is correct.', 'actual': 'Works correctly. The step size is checked and truncated, which shows the inconsistency with the embedded RK integrators.'}]

